---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias41.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Definitions",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Recognition Criteria",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Measurement — Fair Value Model",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Bearer Plants & Land",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Agricultural Produce & Harvest<",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Government Grants Link",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Gains & Losses",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Judgements & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1">
      <span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">
          Why biological assets are measured differently
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          The Conceptual Framework permits current-value measurement bases (fair
          value) where they provide more relevant information than historical
          cost. IAS 41 is one of the clearest applications of this — biological
          transformation makes historical cost a poor proxy for an asset's
          economic reality.
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">
          HOW BIOLOGICAL ASSETS MAP TO THE FRAMEWORK'S ELEMENTS
        </div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">Control</strong> — the entity controls
            the living animal or plant, typically through ownership, herding, or cultivation
            rights</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">Past event</strong> — acquisition (purchase
            or birth) of the animal/plant</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">Future economic benefit</strong> — cash
            flows from sale of the asset itself or of the agricultural produce it
            yields</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          IAS 41 departs sharply from the historical-cost default used elsewhere
          in IFRS (e.g. IAS 16, IAS 38) because biological transformation —
          growth, degeneration, production, procreation — continuously changes
          the asset's value in ways cost cannot capture.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IAS 41</div>
        <div class="principle">
          The Conceptual Framework's "current value" basis is IAS 41's DEFAULT —
          a rare case in IFRS where fair value, not cost, is the primary
          measurement model.
        </div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;">
            <span>Framework basis</span><span>Used by IAS 41 for…</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;">
            <span>Fair value less costs to sell</span><span
              >Biological assets and agricultural produce at point of harvest
              (DEFAULT)</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;">
            <span>Historical cost</span><span
              >Only where fair value cannot be measured reliably (rare, and only
              on initial recognition)</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;">
            <span>Cost model (IAS 16-style)</span><span
              >Bearer plants (moved to PPE scope since 2016 amendment)</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600"
            >Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong
          > IAS 41 prioritises <strong style="font-weight:600">relevance</strong
          > over the verifiability that historical cost would offer — users need to
          see current herd/crop value to assess an agricultural entity's performance,
          even though fair value estimates for immature biological assets can involve
          significant judgement.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2">
      <span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 41 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">
          IAS 41 applies to agricultural activity — the management by an entity
          of the biological transformation and harvest of biological assets for
          sale, or for conversion into agricultural produce or additional
          biological assets — when it results in:
        </div>
        <div
          style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:5px;"
        >
          (a) biological assets, except bearer plants
        </div>
        <div
          style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:5px;"
        >
          (b) agricultural produce AT THE POINT OF HARVEST
        </div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);">
          (c) government grants related to biological assets measured at fair
          value less costs to sell
        </div>
        <div class="elabel" style="margin-top:9px;">
          SCOPE EXCLUSIONS — GOVERNED BY OTHER STANDARDS
        </div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span
            >Bearer plants (e.g. tea bushes, rubber trees, grape vines) → <strong
              style="font-weight:600">IAS 16</strong
            > (produce growing on bearer plants stays within IAS 41 until harvest)</span
          >
          <span
            >Land related to agricultural activity → <strong
              style="font-weight:600">IAS 16</strong
            > / <strong style="font-weight:600">IAS 40</strong></span
          >
          <span
            >Intangible assets related to agricultural activity (e.g. quotas,
            licences) → <strong style="font-weight:600">IAS 38</strong></span
          >
          <span
            >Agricultural produce AFTER the point of harvest → <strong
              style="font-weight:600">IAS 2</strong
            > (Inventories) — harvested produce becomes inventory measured at the
            IAS 41 fair-value-at-harvest amount, which then becomes its "cost" under
            IAS 2</span
          >
          <span
            >Right-of-use assets over land used in agricultural activity → <strong
              style="font-weight:600">IFRS 16</strong
            ></span
          >
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Biological asset</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A living animal or plant.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Agricultural produce</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">
            The harvested product of the entity's biological assets (e.g. milk,
            wool, harvested cane, picked fruit) — measured under IAS 41 only up
            to the point of harvest.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Biological transformation</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">
            The processes of growth, degeneration, production, and procreation
            that cause qualitative or quantitative changes in a biological
            asset.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Bearer plant</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">
            A living plant that is used in production/supply of agricultural
            produce, expected to bear produce for more than one period, and has
            a remote likelihood of being sold as agricultural produce itself
            (excludes annual crops).
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Costs to sell</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">
            Incremental costs directly attributable to the disposal of an asset,
            excluding finance costs and income taxes (e.g. commissions, levies,
            transfer taxes).
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — RECOGNITION CRITERIA ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3">
      <span class="num">3</span><h2>Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"
      ></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">
          The recognition test for biological assets and agricultural produce
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          A biological asset or item of agricultural produce is recognised ONLY
          when ALL THREE conditions are met — mirroring the general Framework
          recognition criteria, adapted for living assets.
        </div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">
              Condition 1 — Control
            </div>
            <div
              style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;"
            >
              The entity controls the asset as a result of past events (e.g.
              legal ownership of a herd, or contractual control over livestock).
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">
              Condition 2 — Probable future economic benefit
            </div>
            <div
              style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;"
            >
              It is probable that future economic benefits associated with the
              asset will flow to the entity.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-box" style="margin-top:7px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">
            Condition 3 — Reliable measurement
          </div>
          <div
            style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;"
          >
            The fair value or cost of the asset can be measured reliably.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600">Newborn/young animals</strong> are recognised
          as soon as they meet the recognition criteria — e.g. a calf is recognised
          at birth, initially measured at fair value less costs to sell, generating
          a gain in the period of birth.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — MEASUREMENT: FAIR VALUE MODEL (with worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4">
      <span class="num">4</span><h2>Measurement — Fair Value Model</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The core measurement formula</div>
        <div class="formula-box formula-box--chip">
          <div class="f-label">
            DEFAULT MEASUREMENT — INITIAL AND SUBSEQUENT
          </div>
          <div class="f-eq">Carrying amount = Fair value − Costs to sell</div>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">
          FAIR VALUE HIERARCHY (IFRS 13 PRINCIPLES APPLIED)
        </div>
        <div class="dot-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">Best evidence</strong> — quoted price
            in an active market for the identical asset</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">No active market</strong> — most recent
            market transaction price, market prices for similar assets adjusted for
            differences, or sector benchmarks</span
          >
          <span
            ><strong style="font-weight:600">No reliable fair value</strong> (rare,
            only at initial recognition) — measure at cost less accumulated depreciation
            and impairment, until fair value becomes reliably measurable</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600"
            >Never deduct transport/selling costs to get the asset to market</strong
          > from fair value — only INCREMENTAL costs directly attributable to disposal
          (e.g. broker commissions) qualify as "costs to sell." Transport costs to
          get produce TO a market are excluded from costs to sell.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — dairy herd at year end</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Fair value gain on a dairy herd</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            An entity's dairy herd had a fair value less costs to sell of
            $400,000 at the start of the year. During the year, 15 calves were
            born (fair value less costs to sell at birth: $18,000 total), and
            the herd's fair value less costs to sell at year end (excluding the
            newly born calves, and after accounting for their subsequent growth)
            is $455,000, inclusive of the calves.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Total gain</strong> = Closing FV less costs to sell − Opening
            FV less costs to sell<br />
            = 455,000 − 400,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);"
              >$55,000</strong
            ><br />
            This $55,000 combines the gain from births ($18,000) and the gain from
            price changes and physical growth of the existing herd ($37,000) — IAS
            41 does not require these to be split, though many entities disclose the
            split voluntarily.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">JOURNAL ENTRY</div>
        <div class="entry">
          <span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">
            Biological assets — dairy herd $55,000<div class="badges">
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">
            Gain on fair value re-measurement $55,000<div class="badges">
              <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          Unlike PPE revaluations, fair value gains/losses on biological assets
          ALWAYS go through profit or loss — never OCI. There is no revaluation
          reserve concept in IAS 41.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — BEARER PLANTS & LAND ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5">
      <span class="num">5</span><h2>
        Bearer Plants &amp; Land — The IAS 16 Carve-Out
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">
          Why bearer plants moved to IAS 16 (2016 amendment)
        </div>
        <div
          style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;"
        >
          Bearer plants (e.g. tea bushes, oil palms, grapevines, rubber trees)
          operate more like a manufacturing asset than a biological asset being
          grown for sale — they are held solely to grow produce over multiple
          periods, similar to a machine. The IASB concluded their fair value
          fluctuations added cost and complexity without commensurate benefit to
          users.
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">
          BEARER PLANT — ALL THREE CONDITIONS MUST BE MET
        </div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Used in the production or supply of agricultural produce</span>
          <span>Expected to bear produce for more than one period</span>
          <span
            >Has a remote likelihood of being sold as agricultural produce,
            except for incidental scrap sales</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600"
            >Annual crops are NOT bearer plants</strong
          > — e.g. wheat, maize, sugar cane remain within IAS 41 throughout, since
          the whole plant is typically harvested/consumed. Only PERENNIAL producing
          plants qualify.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement split — plant vs produce</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Item</span><span>Governing standard &amp; model</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
            <span>The bearer plant itself (e.g. the tea bush)</span><span
              >IAS 16 — cost model (or revaluation model) with depreciation</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
            <span
              >Produce growing on the bearer plant (e.g. unpicked tea leaves)</span
            ><span>IAS 41 — fair value less costs to sell, until harvest</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Land the bearer plants grow on</span><span
              >IAS 16 (owned) or IAS 40 (if held for capital
              appreciation/rental)</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Harvested tea leaves</span><span
              >IAS 41 fair value at harvest becomes IAS 2 "cost" thereafter</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          A single agricultural operation can therefore straddle three standards
          simultaneously: IAS 16 for the bearer plant and land, IAS 41 for
          unharvested produce, and IAS 2 for harvested produce — a frequently
          tested classification exercise.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — AGRICULTURAL PRODUCE & HARVEST ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6">
      <span class="num">6</span><h2>
        Agricultural Produce &amp; the Point of Harvest
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The point-of-harvest rule</div>
        <div class="formula-box formula-box--chip">
          <div class="f-eq">
            Agricultural produce is measured at FV less costs to sell AT THE
            POINT OF HARVEST — this amount then becomes its deemed "cost" for
            IAS 2 purposes going forward
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;"
        >
          Once harvested, produce leaves IAS 41's scope entirely. Any subsequent
          processing (e.g. milk into cheese, grapes into wine) is a
          manufacturing/inventory question under IAS 2, not an IAS 41 question.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">
          Worked example — harvest and transfer to inventory
        </div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Wheat harvest</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A farming entity's standing wheat crop had a carrying amount (fair
            value less costs to sell) of $60,000 immediately before harvest. At
            the point of harvest, the fair value less costs to sell of the
            harvested wheat is $68,000.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Gain on harvest</strong> = 68,000 − 60,000 = <strong
              style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$8,000</strong
            > → recognised in SOPL in the period of harvest.<br />
            The harvested wheat is then transferred to inventory at $68,000, which
            becomes its "cost" under IAS 2 for all subsequent inventory accounting
            (lower of cost and NRV testing, etc.).
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">
            Inventory (harvested wheat) $68,000<div class="badges">
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">
            Biological assets (standing crop) $60,000<div class="badges">
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">
            Gain on harvest $8,000<div class="badges">
              <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          From this point forward, standard IAS 2 rules apply — including
          subsequent write-downs to net realisable value if market prices fall.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — GOVERNMENT GRANTS LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7">
      <span class="num">7</span><h2>
        Government Grants Linkage (IAS 20 vs IAS 41)
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">
          How IAS 41 overrides IAS 20 for fair-value biological assets
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          Government grants related to biological assets measured at fair value
          less costs to sell are NOT accounted for under IAS 20's
          deferred-income model — IAS 41 has its own, simpler rule based on
          conditionality.
        </div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">UNCONDITIONAL GRANT</div>
            <div
              style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;"
            >
              Recognised in profit or loss as income when — and only when — the
              grant becomes RECEIVABLE.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">CONDITIONAL GRANT</div>
            <div
              style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;"
            >
              Recognised in profit or loss only when the conditions attaching to
              the grant are met (e.g. "farm this land organically for 5 years")
              — if conditions permit part of a grant to be retained before
              conditions are fully met, that part is recognised as conditions
              are satisfied.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          For biological assets still measured at COST (the rare case where fair
          value cannot be reliably determined), the normal IAS 20 rules apply
          instead.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — GAINS & LOSSES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8">
      <span class="num">8</span><h2>
        Gains &amp; Losses — Recognition Mechanics
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">
          Sources of gain/loss and their SOPL treatment
        </div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span
            >Initial recognition of a biological asset at FV less costs to sell
            (e.g. a newborn animal) → gain recognised immediately</span
          >
          <span
            >Initial recognition of agricultural produce at the point of harvest
            → gain/loss recognised immediately</span
          >
          <span
            >Change in FV less costs to sell of biological assets during the
            period → gain/loss recognised immediately</span
          >
          <span
            >Costs to sell are always deducted in arriving at the net
            measurement — never presented separately as an expense</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">Because ALL fair value movements hit SOPL immediately, agricultural
          entities can show highly volatile profit figures driven by commodity
          price swings and biological growth cycles — a common analytical/ethics
          discussion point in exams.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — combined movement analysis</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">
            Example 3: Analysing a herd's total movement
          </div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            Opening herd FV less costs to sell: $200,000. During the year:
            purchases of new stock $50,000 (at FV on acquisition date), sales of
            stock at carrying amount of $30,000 (proceeds $34,000 recognised
            separately as sale proceeds, not through the biological asset
            account), and closing FV less costs to sell (remaining herd):
            $235,000.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Fair value gain for the year</strong> = Closing balance − Opening
            balance − Purchases + Disposals (at carrying amount)<br />
            = 235,000 − 200,000 − 50,000 + 30,000 = <strong
              style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$15,000</strong
            > gain from price/growth movements.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          A herd reconciliation (opening balance, purchases, sales, fair value
          gains, closing balance) is the classic exam proforma for this standard
          — practise building it as a T-account or roll-forward schedule.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9">
      <span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">
          Required disclosures for biological assets and agricultural produce
        </div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GENERAL DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span
                >Aggregate gain/loss on initial recognition and from fair value
                changes during the period</span
              >
              <span
                >Description of each group of biological assets, distinguishing
                consumable from bearer, and mature from immature where relevant</span
              >
              <span
                >Methods and significant assumptions applied in determining fair
                value of each group</span
              >
              <span
                >A reconciliation of changes in carrying amount (purchases,
                sales, harvest, fair value changes, business combinations, FX)</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">ADDITIONAL / ENCOURAGED DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span
                >Existence and carrying amounts of biological assets whose title
                is restricted, and those pledged as security</span
              >
              <span
                >Amount of commitments for the development or acquisition of
                biological assets</span
              >
              <span
                >Financial risk management strategies related to agricultural
                activity</span
              >
              <span
                >Non-financial measures or estimates of physical quantities of
                biological assets held and produce output (encouraged, not
                required)</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10">
      <span class="num">10</span><h2>
        Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span
                >Fair value of biological assets lacking an active/observable
                market (discounted cash flow models, adjusted comparable prices)</span
              >
              <span
                >Point at which "harvest" is deemed to occur for
                continuous-output assets (e.g. milk, which is harvested
                continuously)</span
              >
              <span
                >Costs to sell — distinguishing incremental disposal costs from
                costs that should stay in the FV estimate</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span
                >Whether a plant meets ALL THREE bearer plant conditions
                (borderline for some multi-use crops)</span
              >
              <span>Classifying grants as conditional vs unconditional</span>
              <span
                >Whether reliable fair value measurement is genuinely
                unavailable (a very high bar — rebuttable only in rare, clearly
                evidenced cases)</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">The presumption that fair value CAN be reliably measured is
          rebutted only for biological assets with NO active market and NO
          reliable alternative estimates — and this presumption, once rebutted,
          must be reassessed every period, since it can only apply at initial
          recognition.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IFRS vs US GAAP ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11">
      <span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 41 vs US GAAP (ASC 905)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);"
              >IFRS (IAS 41)</span
            ><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Default measurement model</span><span
              >Fair value less costs to sell</span
            ><span
              >Historical cost is generally used; fair value permitted mainly
              for certain harvested crops/livestock held for sale at market
              price with no material disposal costs</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Bearer plants</span><span
              >Scoped out to IAS 16 (cost/revaluation model, depreciated)</span
            ><span
              >No equivalent specific "bearer plant" concept — generally
              accounted for as PPE-type assets under general US GAAP principles</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Fair value gains recognition</span><span
              >Always through SOPL</span
            ><span
              >Generally not applicable, since fair value is not the default;
              where used, gains typically flow through earnings similarly</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;">
            <span>Dedicated standard</span><span
              >Single comprehensive standard (IAS 41)</span
            ><span
              >No single unified standard — guidance is scattered across
              industry-specific ASC topics (e.g. agriculture-specific SEC/AICPA
              guidance)</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">
          This is one of the widest IFRS/US GAAP measurement gaps in the whole
          standard-setting landscape — IFRS's fair-value-first approach for
          biological assets has no direct US GAAP counterpart, making
          agricultural entities' reported profits and asset values difficult to
          compare across the two frameworks without adjustment.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING TABLE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12">
      <span class="num">12</span><h2>
        Statement Mapping — Where Every Agricultural Event Lands
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">
          Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table
        </div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span
            ><span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Purchase of a biological asset</span><span
              ><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span>Recognised at fair value less costs to sell</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Birth of an animal (initial recognition)</span><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span>Gain recognised immediately, no exceptions</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span
              >Fair value change during the period (price and/or physical
              growth)</span
            ><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span>Always through profit or loss — never OCI</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Harvest of agricultural produce</span><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span
              >Gain/loss recognised, then produce reclassified to inventory</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Sale of a biological asset or harvested produce</span><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span
              >Recognised per IFRS 15/IAS 2 principles once transferred out of
              biological asset classification</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Bearer plant depreciation</span><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>
              <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span
            ><span>Governed by IAS 16, not IAS 41</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Unconditional government grant becoming receivable</span><span
              ><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span
            ><span>Recognised as income immediately</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Conditional government grant, conditions not yet met</span
            ><span>—</span><span
              >Not yet recognised; deferred until conditions satisfied</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Cash paid to acquire biological assets</span><span
              ><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span
            ><span
              >Investing (or operating, if part of routine herd trading)
              activities outflow</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Proceeds from sale of biological assets/produce</span><span
              ><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span
            ><span
              >Investing or operating activities inflow, depending on nature of
              the entity's business</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Unrealised fair value gain add-back</span><span
              ><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span
            ><span
              >Added back/deducted in operating activities under indirect method
              (non-cash item)</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;">
            <span>Group-level reconciliation and valuation disclosures</span
            ><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span
              >Required for each group of biological assets</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13">
      <span class="num">13</span><h2>
        Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map
      </h2><span class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">
          How IAS 41 connects across the IFRS body of literature
        </div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 16</span><span
              >Governs bearer plants and land used in agricultural activity,
              applying the cost/revaluation and depreciation framework instead
              of fair value</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 2</span><span
              >Takes over measurement of agricultural produce immediately AFTER
              harvest, using the IAS 41 fair value at harvest as deemed cost</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 20</span><span
              >Provides the general government grant framework, but is
              OVERRIDDEN by IAS 41's own rules for grants tied to
              fair-value-measured biological assets</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IFRS 13</span><span
              >Supplies the fair value measurement hierarchy and definition
              (exit price, market participants) that IAS 41 relies on for
              determining fair value less costs to sell</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 40</span><span
              >Land held for capital appreciation or rental in connection with
              agricultural activity may instead be classified as investment
              property</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IFRS 15</span><span
              >Governs revenue recognition on the eventual SALE of biological
              assets or agricultural produce, once they leave IAS 41/IAS 2
              measurement</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 1</span><span
              >Determines presentation and classification (typically current or
              non-current split for biological assets depending on operating
              cycle) on the SOFP</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 12</span><span
              >Fair value gains on biological assets can create temporary
              differences where tax bases differ from accounting carrying
              amounts, generating deferred tax</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;">
            <span>IAS 36</span><span
              >Applies to bearer plants (as PPE under IAS 16) for impairment
              testing — biological assets measured at fair value are NOT
              separately impairment tested since fair value already reflects
              current conditions</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14">
      <span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span
        class="line"></span>
    </div>
    <Glossary
      entries={[
        {
          term: "Agricultural activity",
          definition:
            "The management by an entity of the biological transformation and harvest of biological assets for sale, or for conversion into agricultural produce or additional biological assets.",
        },
        {
          term: "Group of biological assets",
          definition:
            'An aggregation of similar living animals or plants (e.g. "dairy cattle," "orange trees") used for disclosure and fair value estimation purposes.',
        },
        {
          term: "Consumable biological assets",
          definition:
            "Assets to be harvested as agricultural produce or sold as biological assets themselves (e.g. livestock for meat, crops).",
        },
        {
          term: "Bearer biological assets",
          definition:
            'Assets held to bear produce repeatedly, other than as agricultural produce themselves (e.g. dairy cattle for milk, sheep for wool) — distinct from "bearer plants," which are scoped OUT to IAS 16 entirely.',
        },
        {
          term: "Active market",
          definition:
            "A market with homogeneous items, willing buyers/sellers, and publicly available prices — the primary source of fair value evidence.",
        },
        {
          term: "Point of harvest",
          definition:
            "The moment produce is detached from the biological asset (or, for continuous production like milk, the moment of milking) — the boundary between IAS 41 and IAS 2.",
        },
      ]}
    />

<FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
